
<p><a href="https://unsung.aresluna.org/ive-spent-years-on-it-years-on-a-box/">Via Marcin Wichary</a>:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Designer Pablo Stanley created <a href="https://composer.pablostanley.com/">a really fun interactive
exploration</a> of what it takes to create a seemingly simple
“composer” (industry’s jargon for a more sophisticated input field
or things like it). [...]</p>

<p>Also, I am burying the lede: it’s a really enjoyable and
well-crafted experience — don’t miss the sound and <em>music!</em> — and this unique way of talking about interface details could be
inspiring in and of itself.</p>

<p>You can also watch the entire thing <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8q0pwh98qmU">as a video</a> with Stanley
narrating, but I personally liked the interactive version more.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>At first I enjoyed this a lot. I got maybe a quarter of the way through it before I thought it was too long — and then poked around and figured out that I wasn’t even halfway done with it yet. At first, yes, it brings to life just how many details a designer might have to think about. But after a bit it’s just neurotic. I drew this conclusion before I looked up <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/pablostanley1/">where Stanley works</a>, which I would have won a bet on.</p>

<p>At the point where I began wondering if this whole thing was a prank of some sort, all I could think was: this is why platforms need a HIG; why the HIG needs to be thorough, thoughtful, and good; and why designers ought to follow the platform’s HIG.</p>

